==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|la|civilitas}}: compare {{cog|fr|civilité}}. [[English]] equivalent {{suf|en|civil|ity}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/sɪˈvɪl.ɪ.ti/}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-civility.wav|a=Southern England}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# Speech or behaviour that is fit for [[civil]] interactions; [[politeness]], [[courtesy]]. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Swift Gulliver|part=I|page=43|passage={{smallcaps|This}} is an exact Inventory of what we found about the Body of the ''Man-Mountain'', who uſed us with great '''Civility''', and due Reſpect to your Majefty's Commiſſion. }}
#* '''December 1749''' {{w|Philip Stanhope, 4th Earl of Chesterfield}}, ''letter to his son''
#*: The insolent '''civility''' of a proud man is, if possible, more shocking than his rudeness could be.
# {{lb|en|chiefly|in plural}} An individual act or expression of polite [[behaviour]]; a [[courtesy]]. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Richardson Clarissa|volume=I|chapter=III|passage=Mr Lovelace received from every one those '''civilities''' which were due to his birth {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|now|archaic}} The state or fact of being [[civilized]]; [[civilization]]. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1612|author={{w|John Davies (poet)|John Davies}}|title=Discoverie of the True Causes why Ireland was never entirely subdued
|passage=Monarchies have risen from barbarism to '''civility''', and fallen again to ruin.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[civil]] [[office]]; a civil [[capacity]]. {{defdate|16th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Latimer Lords Prayer|part=2|chapter=Edward VI 2|folio=36|passage=For what an enormity is this in a chriſtian realme to ſerue in a '''ciuility''', hauinge the profyt of a Prouoſtſhip and a Deanrye, and a Perſonage? But I wil tel you what is lyke to come of it. It wil bring the clergy ſhortly into a very ſlauery.}}

